Ou QApplication::computeMask() is not thread-safe. It is safe to create a QImage from a QPixmap in a thread, but accessing the QImage data directly from multiple threads can lead to undefined behavior. To safely manipulate QImage data in a multi-threaded environment, you should ensure that any access to the QImage is protected by mutexes.

To resolve the crash in your code, you can use a mutex to synchronize access to the QImage. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to use a mutex:

```cpp
#include <QImage>
#include <QMutex>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cassert>

class ImageProcessor {
public:
    ImageProcessor() : imageSource(0, 0, QImage::Format_ARGB32) {}

    void setImageSize(int width, int height) {
        QImage newImage(width, height, QImage::Format_ARGB32);
        QMutex mutex;
        // Copy the old image if there is one
        if (!imageSource.isNull()) {
            mutex.lock();
            for (int x = 0; x < imageSource.width(); ++x) {
                for (int y = 0; y < imageSource.height(); ++y) {
                    imageSource.setPixel(x, y, newImage.pixel(x, y));
                }
            }
            mutex.unlock();
        }
        imageSource = newImage;
    }

private:
    QImage imageSource;
};

int main() {
    ImageProcessor processor;
    int width = 800;  // example width
    int height = 600; // example height
    processor.setImageSize(width, height);  // This should not crash now
    // ... rest of your code ...
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, a mutex `mutex` is created and used to synchronize the access to `QImage`. The `setImageSize` function locks the mutex before copying the pixels from the old image to the new image, ensuring that the QImage is accessed safely from multiple threads.

